So usually these are young students, but every now and then we get somebody who visits Stanford. So this guy, his name's Michael Brennan. He was on leave from being the CEO of the United Way of Southeastern Michigan. First conversation I have, he pulls out this really long form and lays it out on the floor. It's 42 pages long. It has a thousand questions. Wow. And he says, this form is a form that is completed by more than 2 million Michigan residents a year, people who need help with food, with medical care, with financial assistance.

33
00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:57.000
And it's just, it's a terrible form. And just to give you an example of how silly the form was, my favorite or least favorite question was, when was your child conceived? Conceived, not born. Conceived, not born, conceived, not born. And he starts a nonprofit called Sevilla in southeastern Michigan. And one of their first projects was to work on trying to fix this form. And they actually made a huge amount of change in this.

