many of them have done so by actually introducing friction into the system. If you think about the civil rights movement, for example, you might have marches and protests that basically make it hard for organizations or societies to function smoothly. In some ways, introducing friction, throwing sand into the gears, is designed to say there's something wrong with the way the gears are turning. Let's slow down and figure out what needs to be fixed. Well, I mean, that's interesting. I'm not a social movement theorist, but my co-author, Hagi Rau, is.

